      "pronunciationText": "Last time, potassium burst into a lilac flame on water. Now put a drop of potassium chloride solution into a Bunsen flame. The sample isn't burning, but the flame turns lilac. Sodium compounds turn it yellow-orange. So the colour belongs to the metal. Chemists use this in a flame test. But how much can a flame colour really tell you, and what can't it identify reliably?",
