      ],
      "requestAttempts": 0,
      "pronunciationText": "Here's the second problem. Sodium compounds are almost everywhere, in dust, on skin, in tap water, even on glassware. And sodium's yellow-orange is very bright. So a tiny trace of sodium can swamp a weaker colour, like potassium's pale lilac. In a mixture, the strongest colour tends to win. That's why the blank, and a clean loop, really matter.",
